A||||5||Gokart||Battery storage FYI||||4||
Z||000000||Gokart||01-16-2000||03:21 PM||||Well, the snow is here so my GT will be off the road till the snows all gone. Pulled the battery and had it downstairs on the floor of my basement and a friend stopped by and told me it will be drained if I leave it there!?! Said the concrete draws it dry! Just thought others might want to know this or is it something I should've already known!!||||reg||4
Z||000001||cobra427||01-16-2000||04:13 PM||||I have always heard that as well. If you are going to leave it out for an extended period of time, you might want to buy one of those 1 amp trickle chargers to kind of keep it "topped off". just a thought ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||01-16-2000||04:33 PM||||Back in my day that was true but now with the improved case materials I don't believe that it's a problem any more.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||01-16-2000||05:32 PM||||I've heard that same thing for many years. I've never stored one that way long so I can't say for sure if it's true or not. I always throw it on a block of scrap wood just in case it is. It's cheap insurance. Personally I don't see how it's posible.<P>Lead Acid batteries have an operating range of charge they like. Unlike a NiCad that likes being totally drained, if you do that to a lead acid battery you will ruin it. In the case of car batteries that range is about 9.5-13.5 volts.<P>And of course they hate over charging. You want to put them on some type of charger that cannot over charge the battery. preferably a charger that has a bit of smarts, not just a straight trickle charger.<P>Also from dealling with "Uninteruptable Power Supply" systems for computers for many years, I've learned that lead acids like to be exercized fairly regular. An easy way to do this with a car battery would be to take a headlight bulb and let it load the battery for awhile. watch it with a volt meter, and when the battery drops to about10.5-11 volts put it back on the charger. Doing this every couple weeks will help prevent cell shorts.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||01-16-2000||06:48 PM||||I had also heard of the concrete battery thing. Several months ago I started asking around. It turns out this was true(back in Phill's day  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) when the battery case was made of rubber. Rubber is a porous material, allowing the charge to run to ground, draining the battery. With todays batteries being made with a plastice case, keeping them off cement for extended periods is no longer an issue. The longest I have kept a battery out of a car and on cement was one month. After popping the battery back in the car, there was no noticeable drain. Engine cranked just a fast as usuall.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||01-16-2000||11:29 PM||||I had forgotten about the old rubber cases..... Concrete could be a problem with those. so could other things. Been so long since I saw a rubber case it's not funny. But then when your 306 years old you won't remember them either.......||||reg||1
